From: EglantinePS5 <noreply@eglantineps5.local>
Subject: [PATCH] Migrate 0332-frizti-02-oot-dynamic-fps.patch

Source: 0332-frizti-02-oot-dynamic-fps.patch
loader: Dynamic FPS for Ocarina of Time at load (EDEN_NX2_OOT_DYNAMIC_FPS=1, off by default)

Core patch. Paths are the submodule's. Applies on core16 alone. Does not need lichii-shaders-0202.

Source: Frizti/Lichii 765659af4c (Frizti), GPL-3.0-or-later. The patch itself is by Dark (Eglantine
Dev); Frizti's commit says he bundled it with Dark's permission. If next17 is ever published, ask Dark
too. Only a 4-byte call and a 120-byte routine are in the source: no game data.

The game advances by a fixed step each frame, so at run 62's 42 ms frames it plays at about 80% speed.
That step, 1/30 s, is what Frizti's commit says; nobody here has read it from the game.
With the switch its step follows the measured time. This adds no frames. It makes the game play at
full speed down to 20 fps; below that the 50 ms cap slows it again.

What the routine does, from its disassembly: repeats the instruction it replaces, reads the system
counter, caps the frame time at 50 ms, smooths it by 1/8, uses it if it is over 3% above the game's
own step. Two words of state sit near the module's end, main+0x4e9fe00. Run 62's module is 0x4ea0000
long, so they are inside it and start at zero (the console's loader zeroes everything past the data).
That they lie past the BSS is inferred, not read: the log line prints where the BSS ends, and it must
be at or below 0x4e9fe00. The routine reads CNTPCT and CNTFRQ, and both follow the 31.25 MHz guest
counter of Switch 2 titles (core.cpp, arm_dynarmic_64.cpp; run 62 logs that rate), so the cap stays
50 ms.

Changed from the source: written in the loader, next to the Mario Kart patches, not in the built-in
table. So it needs no mods folder and no second copy of the module, which the console's loader makes
when PatchNSO runs. Refused unless the title, the module and the build id match, and the routine must
lie inside the code segment.

Runs on the PS5: yes. Run 62's main has this build id, E0F3CCFA...3348.
Hang: none added. The log line shows the word replaced; Frizti's table does not check it. Expect
0x1e201828, fdiv s8, s1, s0, the routine's first instruction. Any other word: stop and look.
Risk: game logic at a longer step is only tested on PC. Wall-clock progress is faster, so memory may
run out sooner by the clock, not by the distance walked.
Pool memory: none.
Checked: bytes equal Frizti's; disassembled with llvm-mc; the bl lands on the routine; a model of the
routine gives 100% speed at 24 and 30 fps, 50% at 10 fps. PS5-target syntax compile. The console port's
nso.cpp anchors still match. Not run.

